\section{What the table shows}{

\strong{Count rows.} One row per coefficient of the count component. Under
\code{exponentiate = TRUE} they are rate ratios of the count process: for
\code{zeroinfl}, the rate among units not in the structural-zero class; for
\code{hurdle}, the latent untruncated rate. They are not multiplicative
effects on the overall expected count E(Y).

\strong{Zero component.} Two-part models show their full model, and the
note says what each block models:
\itemize{
\item \code{zeroinfl}, and \code{glmmTMB} with \code{ziformula}: a \code{Zero-inflation}
block, the model for the probability of a structural zero.
\item \code{hurdle}: a \verb{Zero hurdle, Pr(y > 0)} block, the model for the
probability of a nonzero count, the opposite direction, hence the
distinct label.
\item A \code{glmmTMB} hurdle model (a zero-truncated family, \code{ziGamma()},
\code{lognormal()} or \code{beta_family()} with \code{ziformula}): a
\verb{Zero hurdle, Pr(y = 0)} block, the probability of a zero.
\item \code{glmmTMB} with covariates in \code{dispformula}: a \code{Dispersion}
block, on the log scale, never exponentiated. Under \code{beta_family()}
the dispersion parameter is the precision phi, and the block is a
\code{Precision} block, as for a \code{betareg} \code{y ~ x | z}.
}
The component rows carry Wald inference (B, SE, z, p, CI), join the
\code{p_adjust} family and take significance stars.

\strong{AME.} The effect on the overall expected count E(Y), combining the
count and zero processes, computed by \code{marginaleffects::avg_slopes()} on
the full model. The zero-component rows carry no AME of their own,
since the combined effect includes them.
}

