\section{Standard errors, degrees of freedom and intervals}{

The coefficients of both components are tested by Wald z.

\code{"CR1"} for Poisson and geometric \code{pscl} fits comes from
\code{\link[sandwich:vcovCL]{sandwich::vcovCL()}}, which covers both components at once: the CR0
sandwich scaled by \eqn{G/(G-1)}{G/(G-1)}, \code{G} the number of clusters,
the one cluster estimator of these classes. A negative-binomial
\code{zeroinfl} or \code{hurdle} keeps its model-based standard errors: the
sandwich of these fits leaves out the score of theta. When the count
part is negative binomial, fit the model with \code{glmmTMB} instead (family
\code{nbinom2} with \code{ziformula}, or \code{truncated_nbinom2} for a hurdle), whose
sandwich includes it; glmmTMB's zero hurdle is binomial, as with
\code{zero.dist = "binomial"}. A \code{hurdle} whose zero part alone is negative
binomial (\code{zero.dist = "negbin"}) is refused too; refit it with the
default \code{zero.dist = "binomial"}, which keeps \code{"CR1"}.

\code{"CR0"} and \code{"CR1"} for \code{glmmTMB} fits come from glmmTMB's own sandwich
and cover the zero-inflation and dispersion blocks; see
\link{table_regression_mixed}.
}

\section{Fit statistics}{

The dispersion of a negative-binomial fit is an opt-in fit-statistic
row: \code{"theta"} and \code{"alpha"} (= 1/theta, the Stata \code{nbreg} convention)
for NB2 fits, among them \code{zeroinfl} and \code{hurdle} with
\code{dist = "negbin"}, whose theta is that of the count part; \code{"delta"} for
NB1 fits (glmmTMB \code{nbinom1}). A Poisson column in the same table is
blank on these rows. See \emph{Vocabulary tokens} in \code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}}
for the classes each token covers.
}

\section{What is refused, and why}{

\itemize{
\item \verb{HC*}: the \code{pscl} fits have no hat values, so the HC
machinery of \pkg{sandwich} fails on them.
\item A robust \code{vcov} for a \code{pscl} fit with a negative-binomial part
(see above).
\item \code{"CR0"}, \code{"CR2"}, \code{"CR3"}, \code{"bootstrap"} and \code{"jackknife"} for
the \code{pscl} fits: \code{"CR1"} is their one robust estimator.
\item \code{standardized}, for \code{zeroinfl} and \code{hurdle}.
}
}

